  "worked_solution": "Approaching from the left, we have $x<\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=6x+1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{-}}(6x+1) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow,  approaching from the right, we have $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ so we use the expression $f(x)=-4{log}_{2}\u2061x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {(1/2)}^{+}}-4{log}_{2}\u2061x \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}\u2061\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-4{log}_{2}\u2061{2}^{-1} \\\\  & =-4(-1){log}_{2}\u20612 \\\\  & =-4(-1) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the left- and right-sided limits are equal to $4,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=4.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
